'''''Trevesia palmata''''' is a small tree up to {{cvt|6|m}} height. It is in the family [[Araliaceae]], and is native to [[Southeast Asia]],<ref name="POWO">{{cite web | title=Trevesia palmata (Roxb. ex Lindl.) Vis. | website=Plants of the World Online | date=2023-12-19 | url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:92662-1 | access-date=2025-06-16}}</ref><ref name="tropicos">{{cite web|url=http://www.tropicos.org/Name/2201545|title=Tropicos &#124; Name - Trevesia palmata (Mart. & Zucc.) Benth. ex Hook. f.|website=tropicos.org|accessdate=2017-08-03}}</ref> from [[India]] to southern [[China]]. The common name is '''snowflake tree''' <ref name="flowersofindia"> {{cite web|title=Snowflake Tree |url=https://www.flowersofindia.net/catalog/slides/Snowflake%20Tree.html#:~:text=Snowflake%20Tree%20is%20an%20unbranched,at%20the%20ends%20of%20branches |access-date=March 4, 2025}}</ref> The leaves can be very unusual, sometimes with a central leaflet to which as many as nine peripheral leaflets are attached. These leaflets may be lobed to various degrees, each attached to the central leaflet by a [[petiolule]].<ref>{{cite book | last= Hogan | first= Sean | date= 2004 |title= Flora - The Gardener's Bible | location= Willoughby, New South Wales | publisher= Global Book Pub. Pty. Ltd. | page= 1427 with photo |isbn= 1-74048-097-X }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1= Turner | first1= R.J. Jr. | last2= Wasson | first2=Ernie | date= 1999 | title= Botanica | location= not given | publisher= Barnes and Noble | page= 895 | ISBN= 0760716420 }}</ref>

